<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1941 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Maréchal, nous voilà !</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A French song of 1941 praising Marshal Pétain, who ruled under the German occupation; its tune was stolen from a Jewish composer.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>André Montagard</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Kazimierz Oberfeld (melody plagiarised; credited to André Montagard and Charles Courtioux)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Vichy regime in France</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Avignon, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1941</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by André Montagard, 1941; melody by Kazimierz Oberfeld (murdered 1945), 1933)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a>&quot; (&quot;Marshal, here we are!&quot;) is a French propaganda song of 1941 in honour of Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a>, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime, an authoritarian and antisemitic state set up during the German occupation. According to Guy Guenoun, son of the Jewish founder of &quot;Mon Bar&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>, the songwriter and poet André Montagard wrote the words at a table there in 1941 to enter a competition the Vichy regime had organised to give Pétain an official song; Guenoun recalled that Montagard, often short of money, was moved more by the promised prize than by belief in the regime. It was published by the Ver Luisant music house, run by Rolf Marbot (born Albrecht Marcuse), which issued other songs in praise of Pétain; Charles Courtioux, the firm's printer of sheet music, signed the music with Montagard.</p><p>The melody was plagiarised from a tune written by the Polish Jewish composer Kazimierz (Casimir) Oberfeld for the 1933 film operetta &quot;La Margoton du bataillon&quot;. When the song was declared to SACEM, the French authors' society noted its &quot;obvious kinship&quot; with Oberfeld's music and warned Courtioux of the &quot;strange similarity&quot;. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>'s antisemitic laws Oberfeld, who had been one of SACEM's members entitled to royalties before the occupation, could no longer register works or receive his royalties because he was a Jew. He was deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> in 1943 and died on a death march in 1945. The same tune had already been borrowed for Frédo Gardoni's &quot;La Fleur au guidon&quot;, written for the 1937 Tour de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> remained the main anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, though not formally named the national anthem; in the southern zone it was often followed by &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a>&quot;, which became the regime's unofficial anthem. André Dassary recorded it for Pathé in 1941, with Marcel Cariven's orchestra, and Andrex also sang it. Radio-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the Radio nationale broadcast it regularly, and it was played across France and the empire, in most schools, in the youth work camps (chantiers de jeunesse), in barracks and at meetings of the Milice. Its refrain shows the cult of personality built around Pétain from 1940.</p><p>As a pillar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> propaganda, the song drew a pamphlet against Pétain and parodies from the Resistance, among them &quot;Général, nous voilà !&quot; in honour of General de Gaulle and Julien Clément's &quot;Maréchal, les voilà !&quot;. It has since been used in film soundtracks to set the period, among them The Chorus (2004), La Rafle (2010), the two War of the Buttons films of 2011 and Fanny's Journey (2016), and the newspaper Libération echoed it in its headline on the death of Jean-Marie Le Pen on 8 January 2025.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A propaganda song of loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a>, part of the personality cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime; rousing and devotional.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="769761" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Andre-dassary-marechal-nous-voila-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
